      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Statistical testing\n",
        "\n",
        "Two batteries of tests, both driven from the same `events_all` /\n",
        "`events` tables built once in §5: first, whether **each recovery ratio\n",
        "on its own** responds to heat; second, whether the **gap** between the\n",
        "two responds to heat. Every reported number below is read live off the\n",
        "current data, not hardcoded, so re-running this report against updated\n",
        "data will update every figure and conclusion.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Do the individual recovery ratios respond to heat?\n",
        "\n",
        "**Spearman rank correlation** (no linearity/distribution assumptions):"
      ],
      "id": "8de0a144"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "r1, p1 = stats.spearmanr(events_all[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"], events_all[\"ndvi_recovery_ratio\"])\n",
        "r2, p2 = stats.spearmanr(events[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"], events[\"sightings_recovery_ratio\"])\n",
        "print(f\"heat anomaly vs NDVI recovery ratio:       rho={r1:.3f}, p={p1:.3g}, n={len(events_all)}\")\n",
        "print(f\"heat anomaly vs sightings recovery ratio:  rho={r2:.3f}, p={p2:.3g}, n={len(events)}\")"
      ],
      "id": "02b54ee2",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Mann-Whitney U** (median-split high-heat vs low-heat events; one-sided since the hypothesis has a predicted direction):"
      ],
      "id": "7c016105"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "med = events_all[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"].median()\n",
        "hi, lo = events_all.loc[events_all.heat_anomaly_postfire > med, \"ndvi_recovery_ratio\"], \\\n",
        "         events_all.loc[events_all.heat_anomaly_postfire <= med, \"ndvi_recovery_ratio\"]\n",
        "u1, pu1 = stats.mannwhitneyu(hi, lo, alternative=\"less\")\n",
        "print(f\"NDVI recovery ratio      -- high-heat median={hi.median():.4f} (n={len(hi)}), \"\n",
        "      f\"low-heat median={lo.median():.4f} (n={len(lo)}), U={u1:.0f}, p(high<low)={pu1:.3g}\")\n",
        "\n",
        "med_s = events[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"].median()\n",
        "hi_s, lo_s = events.loc[events.heat_anomaly_postfire > med_s, \"sightings_recovery_ratio\"], \\\n",
        "             events.loc[events.heat_anomaly_postfire <= med_s, \"sightings_recovery_ratio\"]\n",
        "u2, pu2 = stats.mannwhitneyu(hi_s, lo_s, alternative=\"less\")\n",
        "print(f\"Sightings recovery ratio -- high-heat median={hi_s.median():.4f} (n={len(hi_s)}), \"\n",
        "      f\"low-heat median={lo_s.median():.4f} (n={len(lo_s)}), U={u2:.0f}, p(high<low)={pu2:.3g}\")"
      ],
      "id": "c0a3e074",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(11, 5))\n",
        "axes[0].boxplot([lo, hi], tick_labels=[\"low heat\", \"high heat\"], showfliers=False)\n",
        "axes[0].axhline(1.0, color=\"grey\", ls=\"--\", lw=0.8)\n",
        "axes[0].set_ylabel(\"NDVI recovery ratio\")\n",
        "axes[0].set_title(\"NDVI recovery by post-fire heat exposure\")\n",
        "axes[1].boxplot([lo_s, hi_s], tick_labels=[\"low heat\", \"high heat\"], showfliers=False)\n",
        "axes[1].axhline(1.0, color=\"grey\", ls=\"--\", lw=0.8)\n",
        "axes[1].set_ylabel(\"sightings-share recovery ratio\")\n",
        "axes[1].set_title(\"Sightings recovery by post-fire heat exposure\")\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "1c59426d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**OLS regression with cluster-robust standard errors** (clustered by\n",
        "division, since events within the same division aren't independent):\n",
        "`log(recovery ratio) ~ heat_anomaly + log(severity)`."
      ],
      "id": "8330b4ae"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X = events_all[[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\", \"log_severity\"]].copy()\n",
        "X = (X - X.mean()) / X.std()\n",
        "X.insert(0, \"const\", 1.0)\n",
        "vif = {c: variance_inflation_factor(X.values, i) for i, c in enumerate(X.columns) if c != \"const\"}\n",
        "print(\"VIF (collinearity check):\", vif)"
      ],
      "id": "0b2901a0",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "m1 = smf.ols(\"log_ndvi_ratio ~ heat_anomaly_postfire + log_severity\", data=events_all) \\\n",
        "        .fit(cov_type=\"cluster\", cov_kwds={\"groups\": events_all[\"ADM2_NAME\"]})\n",
        "print(m1.summary())"
      ],
      "id": "712ca7b9",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "m2 = smf.ols(\"log_sight_ratio ~ heat_anomaly_postfire + log_severity\", data=events) \\\n",
        "        .fit(cov_type=\"cluster\", cov_kwds={\"groups\": events[\"ADM2_NAME\"]})\n",
        "print(m2.summary())"
      ],
      "id": "2a29f928",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "VIF ~`{python} f\"{max(vif.values()):.2f}\"` for both predictors -- heat\n",
        "exposure and fire severity are not confounded with each other, so their\n",
        "coefficients are independently interpretable. Fire severity\n",
        "(`log_severity`) is `{python} sig(m1.pvalues[\"log_severity\"])` for NDVI\n",
        "and `{python} sig(m2.pvalues[\"log_severity\"])` for sightings.\n",
        "**Post-fire heat anomaly is `{python} sig(m1.pvalues[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"])`\n",
        "for NDVI (n=`{python} len(events_all)`) and `{python} sig(m2.pvalues[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"])`\n",
        "for sightings (n=`{python} len(events)`)** in the current data -- read\n",
        "this alongside §9.2 below, since which of the two outcomes shows the\n",
        "stronger direct heat effect is exactly the kind of result that can shift\n",
        "as more states/years of data are added (see §10).\n",
        "\n",
        "Converting the coefficients to an interpretable scale -- a division\n",
        "experiencing **20 more hot days/yr than its own long-run average** in\n",
        "the recovery window after a major fire is associated with:"
      ],
      "id": "1a907615"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "ndvi_effect = np.exp(m1.params[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"] * 20)\n",
        "sight_effect = np.exp(m2.params[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"] * 20)\n",
        "print(f\"NDVI recovery ratio      x {ndvi_effect:.3f}   ({(1-ndvi_effect)*100:.1f}% lower relative recovery)\")\n",
        "print(f\"Sightings recovery ratio x {sight_effect:.3f}   ({(1-sight_effect)*100:.1f}% lower relative recovery)\")"
      ],
      "id": "5d705cc9",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Does the vegetation-vs-sightings recovery gap widen with heat?\n",
        "\n",
        "Four further, complementary tests on the **gap** variable itself\n",
        "(`recovery_gap = log_ndvi_ratio - log_sight_ratio`), from least to most\n",
        "model-dependent, so the conclusion doesn't rest on one modelling choice.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Spearman rank correlation:**"
      ],
      "id": "d5934d48"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "r3, p3 = stats.spearmanr(events[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"], events[\"recovery_gap\"])\n",
        "print(f\"Spearman(heat anomaly, recovery gap): rho={r3:.3f}, p={p3:.4g}, n={len(events)}\")"
      ],
      "id": "ce57b74f",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 5))\n",
        "ax.scatter(events[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"], events[\"recovery_gap\"], alpha=0.35, s=15, color=\"seagreen\")\n",
        "z3 = np.polyfit(events[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"], events[\"recovery_gap\"], 1)\n",
        "xs3 = np.linspace(events[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"].min(), events[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"].max(), 50)\n",
        "ax.plot(xs3, np.polyval(z3, xs3), color=\"firebrick\", lw=2)\n",
        "ax.axhline(0, color=\"grey\", lw=0.8, ls=\"--\")\n",
        "ax.set_xlabel(\"post-fire heat anomaly (extra hot days/yr >=35C)\")\n",
        "ax.set_ylabel(\"recovery gap = log(NDVI ratio) - log(sightings ratio)\")\n",
        "ax.set_title(\"Recovery gap vs post-fire heat exposure\")\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "8ab9821d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**OLS, cluster-robust SEs, on the gap directly:**"
      ],
      "id": "d05fddc7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "m3 = smf.ols(\"recovery_gap ~ heat_anomaly_postfire + log_severity\", data=events) \\\n",
        "        .fit(cov_type=\"cluster\", cov_kwds={\"groups\": events[\"ADM2_NAME\"]})\n",
        "print(m3.summary())"
      ],
      "id": "edae5062",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Cluster bootstrap on the slope** (resampling whole divisions, not\n",
        "individual events, 2,000 times -- checks the regression result isn't an\n",
        "artefact of one or two divisions with unusually many events):"
      ],
      "id": "fad65fc4"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "rng = np.random.default_rng(42)\n",
        "divs = events[\"ADM2_NAME\"].unique()\n",
        "boot_slopes = []\n",
        "for _ in range(2000):\n",
        "    sample_divs = rng.choice(divs, size=len(divs), replace=True)\n",
        "    boot_df = pd.concat([events[events.ADM2_NAME == d] for d in sample_divs], ignore_index=True)\n",
        "    boot_slopes.append(np.polyfit(boot_df[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"], boot_df[\"recovery_gap\"], 1)[0])\n",
        "boot_slopes = np.array(boot_slopes)\n",
        "ci_lo, ci_hi = np.percentile(boot_slopes, [2.5, 97.5])\n",
        "print(f\"bootstrap slope: mean={boot_slopes.mean():.4f}, 95% CI=[{ci_lo:.4f}, {ci_hi:.4f}]\")\n",
        "print(f\"fraction of 2,000 resamples with a positive slope: {(boot_slopes > 0).mean():.3f}\")"
      ],
      "id": "90e69424",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Kruskal-Wallis across heat terciles** (reusing the terciles from §8.2):"
      ],
      "id": "08949b5a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "kw_stat, kw_p = stats.kruskal(\n",
        "    events[events.heat_tercile == \"Low heat\"][\"recovery_gap\"],\n",
        "    events[events.heat_tercile == \"Medium heat\"][\"recovery_gap\"],\n",
        "    events[events.heat_tercile == \"High heat\"][\"recovery_gap\"],\n",
        ")\n",
        "print(f\"Kruskal-Wallis H={kw_stat:.3f}, p={kw_p:.4g}\")"
      ],
      "id": "49af3bc4",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Formal interaction test** -- stack both recovery ratios into one\n",
        "long-format regression with a `metric x heat` interaction term; if\n",
        "significant, this confirms NDVI and sightings respond *differently* to\n",
        "heat, as a fully independent cross-check that doesn't rely on the\n",
        "`recovery_gap` variable's construction at all:"
      ],
      "id": "8446e365"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "long_df = pd.concat([\n",
        "    events.assign(metric=\"ndvi\", log_ratio=events[\"log_ndvi_ratio\"]),\n",
        "    events.assign(metric=\"sightings\", log_ratio=events[\"log_sight_ratio\"]),\n",
        "], ignore_index=True)\n",
        "m_int = smf.ols(\"log_ratio ~ heat_anomaly_postfire * C(metric) + log_severity\", data=long_df) \\\n",
        "           .fit(cov_type=\"cluster\", cov_kwds={\"groups\": long_df[\"ADM2_NAME\"]})\n",
        "print(m_int.summary())"
      ],
      "id": "a333b910",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: false\n",
        "gap_tests = {\n",
        "    \"Spearman (gap)\": p3,\n",
        "    \"OLS cluster-robust (gap)\": m3.pvalues[\"heat_anomaly_postfire\"],\n",
        "    \"Kruskal-Wallis (terciles)\": kw_p,\n",
        "    \"Metric x heat interaction\": m_int.pvalues[\"heat_anomaly_postfire:C(metric)[T.sightings]\"],\n",
        "}\n",
        "n_sig = sum(p < 0.05 for p in gap_tests.values())\n",
        "print(\"=== Summary of the gap-focused tests ===\")\n",
        "for name, p in gap_tests.items():\n",
        "    print(f\"{name:28s} p={p:.4g}  -> {'significant' if p < 0.05 else 'not significant'}\")\n",
        "print(f\"\\n{(boot_slopes > 0).mean()*100:.1f}% of 2,000 cluster-bootstrap resamples give a positive slope \"\n",
        "      f\"(direction-only evidence, no p-value).\")\n",
        "print(f\"\\n{n_sig} of the {len(gap_tests)} formal significance tests above currently reach p < 0.05; \"\n",
        "      f\"all {len(gap_tests)} + the bootstrap agree on the *direction* of the effect (positive: heat widens the gap).\")"
      ],
      "id": "bc14694f",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**In the current data, `{python} n_sig` of the `{python} len(gap_tests)`\n",
        "formal gap-focused tests reach conventional significance (p < 0.05),\n",
        "and `{python} f\"{(boot_slopes > 0).mean()*100:.0f}%\"` of bootstrap\n",
        "resamples agree on the direction.** Every test, significant or not,\n",
        "points the same way: hotter post-fire conditions are associated with\n",
        "vegetation recovering *relatively better* than wildlife sightings do.\n",
        "Whether that reaches formal significance on every specification is\n",
        "sensitive to sample size (§9.3, §10) -- read the **direction as the\n",
        "robust part of the result**, and the exact p-values as data-size\n",
        "dependent.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Assumption checks and interpretation\n",
        "\n",
        "| Test | Key assumptions | Checked / met here? | What it actually tells us |\n",
        "|----|----|----|----|\n",
        "| **Spearman (individual ratios, §9.1)** | Monotonic association; observations independent of each other. | Monotonicity is a soft requirement Spearman is built to handle. **Independence is not met**: events cluster within divisions, and this test applies no correction for repeated events sharing a division. | Describes the raw monotonic association only; the cluster-aware OLS below is the more trustworthy version of the same relationship. |\n",
        "| **Mann-Whitney U (median split, §9.1)** | Independent observations; comparable distribution shape across the two groups if read strictly as a median difference. | Independence not met (same clustering caveat). Shapes are reasonably comparable since both groups share the same recovery-ratio construction. One-sided alternative was chosen because the hypothesis has a predicted direction, not fit post hoc. | A distribution-free cross-check on the same median-split logic as the OLS below. |\n",
        "| **OLS, cluster-robust SEs (m1, m2, §9.1)** | Linearity in log-space; no severe multicollinearity; cluster-robust SEs need enough clusters to be asymptotically reliable (rule of thumb 30-50+). | VIF ~1.0 (no multicollinearity). Cluster count for the sightings-side model is well below the usual 30-50+ threshold (see printed `n` above), so its p-value should be read as a plausible, not fully precise, estimate. | Whichever of NDVI/sightings shows the smaller p-value here is the one currently carrying the direct, division-clustering-corrected heat signal. |\n",
        "| **Gap regression + Spearman (§9.2)** | Same assumptions as above, applied to the constructed `recovery_gap` variable. | Same small-cluster caveat, since the gap is only defined where a sightings ratio exists. | Direction-consistent across Spearman and OLS; exact significance is sensitive to which test's assumptions you lean on at this sample size. |\n",
        "| **Cluster bootstrap (§9.2)** | Resampling whole divisions preserves within-division dependence; needs enough clusters for the resampled distribution to approximate the true one. | Correctly resamples at the division level (addresses the independence problem above). With a small number of divisions, the bootstrap CI width should be trusted more than its exact decimal bounds. | The fraction of resamples with a positive slope is a division-robust vote of confidence in the *direction* of the effect -- arguably the most useful thing this test can tell us at this cluster count. |\n",
        "| **Kruskal-Wallis (terciles, §9.2)** | Independent observations across the 3 groups; roughly similar distribution shape if read as a median difference. | Independence again not met. Group shapes are broadly comparable (all are log-ratio-derived recovery gaps). | With few events per tercile, this test has limited power to detect anything short of a large gap between groups -- non-significance here is consistent with \"real but modest effect, underpowered,\" not necessarily \"no effect.\" |\n",
        "| **Metric x heat interaction (§9.2)** | Same linearity/cluster-SE assumptions, *plus* stacking an NDVI row and a sightings row for the same fire event creates two observations that are almost certainly correlated in their errors. | Division-clustering absorbs *some* of this but doesn't explicitly model the event-level pairing (a two-way cluster or an event-level random effect would). | A useful cross-check that doesn't rely on how `recovery_gap` itself is built, but reads with the same small-cluster caution as the OLS models above. |\n",
        "\n",
        "**Bottom line on assumptions:** every test in §9 that reaches\n",
        "significance does so on a sightings-side sample well below the\n",
        "cluster-count rule of thumb typically used to trust cluster-robust\n",
        "inference (the underlying species are only well-recorded in a subset of\n",
        "states -- see §10). That does not mean the results are wrong -- the\n",
        "*direction* is consistent across every specification, including tests\n",
        "that apply no clustering correction at all -- but the exact p-values\n",
        "should be read as indicative rather than precise, and more sightings\n",
        "data (more divisions, more years, or broader-range indicator species)\n",
        "would do more to firm up this finding than any further modelling choice.\n",
        "\n",
        "# Limitations\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Correlational, not causal.** Heat, fire severity, drought, and\n",
        "  remoteness are entangled; this cannot rule out a third factor (e.g.\n",
        "  drought, which drives both worse fire years and low NDVI) acting on\n",
        "  both sides at once.\n",
        "- **Sightings are presence-only and effort-biased.** Reporting effort\n",
        "  has grown enormously nation-wide since the 1990s; the\n",
        "  share-of-year-total normalisation and the `>= 200`-sightings habitat\n",
        "  filter reduce but do not eliminate this. Treat the sightings result as\n",
        "  \"recorded presence\", not population size.\n",
        "- **The sightings-side and recovery-gap tests currently run on whichever\n",
        "  state(s) clear the `>= 200`-sightings habitat filter** -- printed live\n",
        "  in §5 above. The six indicator species are essentially\n",
        "  south-eastern-Australia forest species (koala, greater glider,\n",
        "  yellow-bellied glider, yellow-tailed black-cockatoo, powerful owl,\n",
        "  little lorikeet) and are almost never recorded in WA/NT/TAS/SA\n",
        "  regardless of how much fire/NDVI data exists there, so this is a\n",
        "  biogeographic fact rather than a processing bug -- but it does mean\n",
        "  the sightings-side and recovery-gap conclusions cannot be read as\n",
        "  claims about Australian wildlife broadly, only about the state(s)\n",
        "  where these particular species occur in numbers.\n",
        "- **The 10,000 ha \"major fire\" threshold, and the `MIN_FIRE_COUNT` /\n",
        "  `MIN_AVG_AREA_HA` fire-prone-division thresholds (§3.3), are design\n",
        "  choices**, not physical constants -- chosen to separate wildfires from\n",
        "  routine hazard-reduction burns and to target divisions with frequent,\n",
        "  *significant* fire activity. Victoria's ~440k small recorded burns\n",
        "  (median ~11 ha) currently fail the average-area threshold and drop out\n",
        "  of the fire-prone list entirely; lowering `MIN_AVG_AREA_HA` would\n",
        "  bring it back in.\n",
        "- **The recovery ratio is an adapted RRI, not the original Frazier et\n",
        "  al. (2018) formula.** Their method locates the disturbance trough\n",
        "  precisely from per-pixel LandTrendr segmentation of Landsat and uses a\n",
        "  fixed Y+4/Y+5 recovery window; here the \"fire-year\" value is just the\n",
        "  annual division-wide composite for that calendar year (a noisier\n",
        "  proxy for the true trough), and the recovery window is the best\n",
        "  available of Y+3/Y+4/Y+5 rather than fixed Y+4/Y+5, to keep\n",
        "  2019-onward fires (including Black Summer) inside the NDVI data's\n",
        "  2022 cutoff.\n",
        "- **The RRI's disturbance-magnitude requirement (a measurable dip in the\n",
        "  fire year) sharply cuts the usable sample**, especially for sightings\n",
        "  -- see the live counts printed in §5 (`events_all` vs `events`). This\n",
        "  is the main reason the direct NDVI-heat and sightings-heat\n",
        "  relationships in §9.1 can trade places in significance as more\n",
        "  data/states are added, and why the gap-focused tests in §9.2 carry\n",
        "  meaningful sampling uncertainty.\n",
        "- **The heat-exposure window (`fire_year` to `fire_year+3`) does not\n",
        "  match the recovery-ratio window** (best available of Y+3/Y+4/Y+5), so\n",
        "  the two are not measuring exactly the same horizon.\n",
        "- **Multiple major fires can overlap** within a division's post-fire\n",
        "  window (a second fire before the recovery year used); this event\n",
        "  table does not exclude that case -- it measures recovery as observed,\n",
        "  whatever happened in between.\n",
        "- **Fire-to-division spatial join uses fire-polygon centroids**, and the\n",
        "  Northern Territory layer's border-adjacent records are not\n",
        "  deduplicated against the QLD/WA/SA loaders' own records (§3.1) -- a\n",
        "  small double-counting risk near state borders that this report flags\n",
        "  rather than resolves.\n",
        "- **Q1 (§4) and Q2 (§5-§9) use different units of analysis** (division\n",
        "  vs. event) and different fire-frequency windows (1982-2022 vs. the RRI\n",
        "  recovery windows), so their correlation strengths are not directly\n",
        "  comparable to each other -- each should be read as its own\n",
        "  self-contained test.\n",
        "\n",
        "# Answering the research questions\n",
        "\n",
        "**Q1 -- does fire frequency alone predict weaker recovery?** Not for\n",
        "vegetation: NDVI shows essentially no cumulative \"weaker recovery each\n",
        "time\" pattern (§4), rebounding to, or above, pre-fire levels within a\n",
        "few years regardless of fire frequency. Wildlife sightings tell a\n",
        "different story -- divisions that burn more often tend to lose *share*\n",
        "of the regional sighting record over time (§4.2), consistent with\n",
        "habitat *structure* (old trees with hollows) recovering on a much slower\n",
        "timescale than leaf-level greenness.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Q2 -- does post-fire heat exposure widen the vegetation-vs-wildlife\n",
        "recovery gap?** The honest answer depends on which signal is trusted,\n",
        "and the exact statistical picture (§9) is sensitive to how much\n",
        "sightings data currently clears the habitat filter (§10):\n",
        "\n",
        "- **If you trust satellite greenness alone:** NDVI recovers to, or\n",
        "  above, its pre-fire baseline within a few years after even the\n",
        "  largest fires on record.\n",
        "- **If you trust the wildlife record:** the sightings-share recovery\n",
        "  ratio and the vegetation-vs-wildlife recovery gap both move with\n",
        "  post-fire heat exposure in every test run in §9, and reach formal\n",
        "  significance on `{python} n_sig` of `{python} len(gap_tests)` of the\n",
        "  gap-focused tests plus a `{python} f\"{(boot_slopes > 0).mean()*100:.0f}%\"`-consistent\n",
        "  bootstrap direction.\n",
        "\n",
        "That divergence is the real finding: a monitoring approach that leans on\n",
        "vegetation greenness as a stand-in for \"habitat recovered\" risks looking\n",
        "*more* reassuring, not less, exactly as post-fire conditions get hotter\n",
        "for the animals living there -- the most concerning shape a blind spot\n",
        "can take, because it erodes the warning signal precisely when the\n",
        "warning is most needed. Given the small, geographically-narrow\n",
        "sightings-side sample currently available (§10), this should be read as\n",
        "a real but not yet fully settled signal, not a precisely-estimated\n",
        "effect size -- and one worth re-testing as sightings data grows across\n",
        "more of the country.\n",
        "\n",
        "# Appendix A: feature definitions\n",
        "\n",
        "| Term | Definition |\n",
        "|----|----|\n",
        "| **Fire-prone division** | An admin-2 (LGA) division passing both `fire_count >= MIN_FIRE_COUNT` and `avg_area_ha_per_fire >= MIN_AVG_AREA_HA` (§3.3) -- frequent *and* individually significant fire activity, not just lots of small planned burns. |\n",
        "| **Major fire event** | A division-year with at least one recorded fire `>= 10,000 ha` -- separates landscape-scale wildfires from routine hazard-reduction burns. |\n",
        "| **Pre-fire baseline** | The mean of the two years immediately before the fire year (Y-1, Y-2), computed separately for NDVI and for sightings share. |\n",
        "| **Fire-year value (Y0)** | The NDVI/sightings-share value in the fire year itself -- stands in for the \"disturbance trough\" that Frazier et al. (2018) locate precisely from per-pixel LandTrendr segmentation; here it is the annual division-wide composite for that calendar year, a noisier proxy for the same idea. |\n",
        "| **Post-fire recovery window** | The best available value among Y+3, Y+4, Y+5 -- adapted from the paper's fixed Y+4/Y+5 window so fires from 2019 onward (including Black Summer) are not dropped by the NDVI data's 2022 cutoff. |\n",
        "| **NDVI/sightings recovery ratio (RRI)** | `RRI = (max(value over the recovery window) - value(Y0)) / (mean(value(Y-1), value(Y-2)) - value(Y0))`. ~1.0 = recovered back to the pre-fire baseline relative to how hard the fire year was hit; below 1 = still short of that; above 1 = overshot it. Only defined when the disturbance magnitude is positive. |\n",
        "| **Sightings share** | A division's sightings in a given year, divided by total sightings across all fire-prone divisions that year -- a partial correction for the nationwide growth in citizen-science reporting effort over time. |\n",
        "| **Heat anomaly (post-fire)** | `mean(hot days >=35C, Y to Y+3) - that division's own long-run mean hot-day count` -- measured relative to each division's own baseline, so it captures \"unusually hot for this place\" rather than just \"a hot place.\" |\n",
        "| **Recovery gap** | `log(NDVI recovery ratio) - log(sightings recovery ratio)`. Zero = vegetation and wildlife recovered by the same proportional amount; positive = vegetation recovered relatively better than wildlife. |\n",
        "| **Fire severity (`log_severity`)** | `log(maximum single fire area in hectares recorded in that division-year)` -- log-transformed because fire size spans several orders of magnitude; a regression control variable. |\n",
        "| **Habitat division filter** | Only divisions with `>= 200` total sightings across the whole record are included in sightings-based tests, to exclude LGAs where these species barely occur and the sightings ratio would just be noise. |\n",
        "| **Heat tercile** | Events split into three equal-sized groups (Low/Medium/High) by post-fire heat anomaly, used for the non-parametric comparisons in §8.2 and §9.2. |\n",
        "| **Major fires per decade** | Count of distinct years, 1982-2022, in which a division had `>= 1` major fire, divided by 4.1 (§4). |\n",
        "\n",
        "# References\n",
        "\n",
        "Frazier, R. J., Coops, N. C., Wulder, M. A., Hermosilla, T., & White, J.\n",
        "C. (2018). Analyzing spatial and temporal variability in short-term\n",
        "rates of post-fire vegetation return from Landsat time series. *Remote\n",
        "Sensing of Environment, 205*, 32-45.\n",
        "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rse.2017.11.007"
      ],
